        "heading": "2 RELATED WORK",
        "text": "Motion Mimicking. To produce realistic animation, motion mimicking is employed to learn skills from motion captures (Hong et al., 2019; Peng et al., 2018a;b; Lee et al., 2019) for physicsbased characters. Motion mimicking enables various downstream tasks in physics-based animation. Learned primitive actions can be re-combined to generate various motions (Peng et al., 2019; Luo et al., 2020), used to achieve a goal (Bergamin et al., 2019; Park et al., 2019; Peng et al., 2021), or leveraged as a pre-train model for general-purpose motor skills (Merel et al., 2018; Hasenclever et al., 2020; Peng et al., 2022; Won et al., 2022; ?). While these downstream tasks look exciting and promising, the scalability can be largely limited by motion mimicking. In our work, we show the problem can be surprisingly easy with differentiable dynamics.\nSpeeding Up Motion Mimicking Most motion mimicking works are based on a DRL framework (Peng et al., 2018a; Bergamin et al., 2019), whose optimization is expensive. Several recent works speed up the DRL process by hyper-parameter searching (Yang & Yin, 2021) and constraint relaxation (Ma et al., 2021). Another line of work learns world models to achieve end-to-end gradient optimization (Won et al., 2022; Fussell et al., 2021). However, learning world models requires extra training and introduces the risk of error accumulation. We are the first to use the off-the-shelf DPS for motion mimicking, which achieves better sample efficiency than optimized DRL frameworks and requires no additional world models.\nDifferentiable Physics Simulators. The emerging differentiable physics simulators (DPS) (Hu et al., 2019; 2020; Huang et al., 2021; Qiao et al., 2021) have brought success in many domains and provide a distinctly new approach to control tasks. Various applications stem from DPS like system identification (Jatavallabhula et al., 2021), continuous controls (Lin et al., 2022; Xu et al., 2022) in the past few years and demonstrate promising results. Compared to black-box counterparts which learn the dynamics with neural networks (Fussell et al., 2021; Li et al., 2020), differentiable simulations utilize physical models to provide more reliable gradients with better interpretability. On the other hand, the analytical gradient from DPS suffers from noise or can sometimes be wrong (Zhong et al., 2022; Suh et al., 2022) in a contact-rich environment. Non-smoothness or discontinuity in the contact event requires specific techniques to compute the gradient. However, they often introduce noise into the gradient (Zhong et al., 2022). Thus, in this work, we empirically show how to handle the noise in the gradient in a contact-rich environment for motion mimicking.\nPolicy Learning with Differentiable Physics Simulators. The recent development of differentiable simulators enables the optimization of control policies via analytical gradient (Mozer, 1989; Mora et al., 2021; Xu et al., 2022). Concurrent to this work, ILD (Chen et al., 2022) proposes a simple yet effective Imitation Learning framework with DPS for robot control. While the first-order gradient largely enhances the sample efficiency for policy learning, challenges lie ahead. The noisy gradient and exploding/vanishing gradients are two crucial issues that hinder the wide deployment of analytical gradient-based reinforcement learning (Degrave et al., 2017). Due to the local nature of the gradient, the policy could be stuck at sub-optimal points. In particular, we observe that ILD-like optimization fails to let characters explore highly dynamic motions. We introduce a simple replay mechanism to alleviate these issues."
